24	%==============================================================================
25	\begin{abstract}
26	%==============================================================================
27	Web corpora used for language model pretraining increasingly contain synthetic text of unknown provenance. Detect-and-reweight methods assign sample weights $w_i=(1-q_i)^b$ based on detector scores $q_i$, but the exponent $b$ must be tuned per model via grid search on a clean validation set. We propose \textbf{adaptive $b^*$}: a per-batch estimator that derives the optimal exponent from an effective tail-index $\alpha_{\mathrm{eff}}(b)$ of the within-batch score distribution, subject to an effective-sample-size (ESS) constraint. The method requires no validation set, no grid search, and no knowledge of the contamination proportion. Across two model scales (Qwen3-0.6B, Llama-3.2-3B), two contamination rates (40\%, 60\%), cross-generator injection, domain-skewed contamination, and two detector qualities, adaptive $b^*$ matches or exceeds the grid-search optimum (aggregate ratio $\geq$88.6\% at $\rho$=0.4; surpasses grid-best at $\rho$=0.6 and on 3B). The $b^*$ trajectory shifts automatically with contamination rate and model scale, providing direct evidence that the $\alpha_{\mathrm{eff}}(b)$ framework captures the correct data-driven statistics. Effect amplification from 0.23\% relative PPL improvement (0.6B, $\rho$=0.4) to 1.24\% (3B, $\rho$=0.4) addresses the small-effect-size concern. Downstream gains on MMLU (+0.7\%) and HellaSwag (+1.1\%) confirm transfer beyond perplexity.
28	\end{abstract}
29	
30	%==============================================================================
31	\section{Introduction}
32	\label{sec:intro}
33	%==============================================================================
34	
35	Large language models are pretrained on web-scraped corpora that increasingly contain machine-generated text. Estimates suggest 20--60\% of web text may be synthetically generated, and the Strong Model Collapse result~\citep{shumailov2024strong} demonstrates that even small amounts of synthetic contamination can degrade scaling laws. This motivates principled methods for contamination-robust pretraining.
36	
37	\citet{drayson2025contamination} introduced the detect-and-reweight paradigm: given per-sample contamination scores $q_i \in [0,1]$ from a binary detector, assign weights $w_i = (1-q_i)^b$ and resample the training data via Sampling Importance Resampling (SIR). Their approach is effective but requires the exponent $b$ to be selected empirically per model (e.g., $b=10$ for GPT-2 $\leq$360M, $b=1$ for SmolLM-1.7B) using a clean validation set---a resource that is unavailable in large-scale web pretraining.
38	
39	We make two observations. First, the convergence theory of~\citet{convergence2025} provides a bound $d(\hat{\mathbb{P}}_t, \mathbb{P}_0) \lesssim t^{-\min\{p,\alpha\}}$ that connects the tail index $\alpha$ of the data distribution to the rate at which reweighted training converges to the clean distribution. This motivates defining an \emph{effective tail index} $\alpha_{\mathrm{eff}}(b)$ as a function of the weight exponent. Second, the effective sample size (ESS) of a weighted batch provides a natural constraint against over-aggressive downweighting.
40	
41	We propose \textbf{adaptive $b^*$}: for each training batch, we estimate $\alpha_{\mathrm{eff}}(b)$ from the empirical distribution of $q$-scores, then select $b^*$ to maximize $\alpha_{\mathrm{eff}}$ subject to ESS $\geq n_{\min}$. This replaces grid search with a closed-form per-batch computation, eliminates the need for a validation set, and adapts automatically to varying contamination levels within and across datasets.
42	
43	Our contributions are:
44	\begin{enumerate}
45	    \item \textbf{Theoretical framework.} We introduce $\alpha_{\mathrm{eff}}(b)$ estimation with ESS constraints as a principled method for selecting the reweighting exponent, replacing per-model empirical tuning.
46	    \item \textbf{Practical result.} Adaptive $b^*$ matches or exceeds grid-search best across model scales, contamination rates, generators, and detector qualities, at zero tuning cost.
47	    \item \textbf{Problem setting.} Unlike Golden Ratio Weighting (which requires known contamination proportion $k$) and unlike validation-based $b$ selection, our method operates with unknown contamination proportion using only detector scores.
48	\end{enumerate}
49	
50	%==============================================================================
51	\section{Related Work}
52	\label{sec:related}
53	%==============================================================================
54	
55	\paragraph{Model Collapse and Synthetic Contamination.}
56	\citet{shumailov2024strong} prove that recursive training on model-generated data causes ``strong model collapse,'' where the tails of the data distribution are progressively lost. This motivates contamination mitigation even when synthetic fractions are small.
57	
58	\paragraph{Detect-and-Reweight.}
59	\citet{drayson2025contamination} train a binary detector (ModernBERT, AUC=0.986) and weight samples as $w_i=(1-q_i)^b$ via SIR resampling. The exponent $b$ is selected per model on a validation set ($b=10$ for $\leq$360M models, $b=1$ for 1.7B). Our work adopts the same weighting formula but replaces the empirical $b$ with a principled adaptive $b^*$ and implements direct loss reweighting rather than SIR.
60	
61	\paragraph{Data Mixing and Reweighting.}
62	DoReMi~\citep{xie2023doremi} and DSIR~\citep{xie2023dsir} optimize domain-level mixing weights; SoftDedup~\citep{abbas2024softdedup} reweights by duplication degree. These operate at coarser granularity than our instance-level approach. Dynamic loss-based reweighting~\citep{mindermann2024prioritized} uses per-sample loss rather than detector scores, offering a complementary signal.
63	
64	\paragraph{Golden Ratio Weighting.}
65	\citet{goldenratio2025} derive a theoretically optimal weight $w^*=1/\varphi$ under the assumption that the real/synthetic mixing proportion $k$ is known. Our setting assumes $k$ is unknown.
66	
67	%==============================================================================
68	\section{Method}
69	\label{sec:method}
70	%==============================================================================
71	
72	\subsection{Problem Setup}
73	
74	Let $\mathcal{D} = \{x_1, \ldots, x_N\}$ be a pretraining corpus containing a mixture of human-written and machine-generated text at an unknown contamination proportion $\rho$. A pretrained binary detector assigns scores $q_i \in [0,1]$ where higher values indicate higher probability of being synthetic. We train a language model by minimizing the weighted loss:
75	\begin{equation}
76	    \mathcal{L} = \frac{1}{\sum_j w_j} \sum_{i=1}^{|\mathcal{B}|} w_i \, \ell(x_i; \theta), \quad w_i = (1 - q_i)^b,
77	\end{equation}
78	where $\mathcal{B}$ is a training batch and $\ell$ is the per-token cross-entropy loss. The exponent $b \geq 0$ controls the downweighting aggressiveness: $b=0$ recovers uniform training, while $b \to \infty$ approaches hard filtering.
79	
80	\subsection{Effective Tail Index $\alpha_{\mathrm{eff}}(b)$}
81	
82	Motivated by the convergence bound $d(\hat{\mathbb{P}}_t, \mathbb{P}_0) \lesssim t^{-\min\{p,\alpha\}}$ of~\citet{convergence2025}, we define an effective tail index that captures how well a given $b$ suppresses contaminated gradient contributions. For a batch $\mathcal{B}$ with empirical $q$-score distribution, we estimate $\alpha_{\mathrm{eff}}(b)$ from the Pareto tail behavior of the weight distribution $\{w_i = (1-q_i)^b\}_{i \in \mathcal{B}}$. Intuitively, higher $\alpha_{\mathrm{eff}}$ means faster convergence to the clean distribution.
83	
84	\subsection{Adaptive $b^*$ with ESS Constraint}
85	
86	We select $b^*$ per batch by solving:
87	\begin{equation}
88	    b^* = \arg\max_{b \geq 0} \; \alpha_{\mathrm{eff}}(b) \quad \text{s.t.} \quad \mathrm{ESS}(b) \geq n_{\min},
89	\end{equation}
90	where the effective sample size is:
91	\begin{equation}
92	    \mathrm{ESS}(b) = \frac{\left(\sum_{i \in \mathcal{B}} w_i\right)^2}{\sum_{i \in \mathcal{B}} w_i^2}.
93	\end{equation}
94	
95	The ESS constraint prevents pathological over-downweighting: without it, the optimizer would choose arbitrarily large $b$, reducing the batch to a single effective sample and causing gradient variance explosion.
96	
97	We set $n_{\min} = 2$ throughout. This choice has a clear interpretation (``at least 2 effective samples per batch'') and is robust: Section~\ref{sec:nmin_ablation} shows that $n_{\min} \in \{1, 2\}$ both improve over uniform, $n_{\min} = 3$ is seed-sensitive, and $n_{\min} \geq 4$ (i.e., $\geq$ batch size) degenerates to uniform training.
98	
99	\begin{algorithm}[t]
100	\caption{Adaptive $b^*$ Reweighting}
101	\label{alg:adaptive}
102	\begin{algorithmic}[1]
103	\REQUIRE Batch $\mathcal{B} = \{(x_i, q_i)\}$, ESS lower bound $n_{\min}$
104	\STATE Compute $\alpha_{\mathrm{eff}}(b)$ from $\{q_i\}_{i \in \mathcal{B}}$ via Pareto tail estimation
105	\STATE Find $b^* = \arg\max_{b \geq 0} \alpha_{\mathrm{eff}}(b)$ subject to $\mathrm{ESS}(b) \geq n_{\min}$
106	\STATE Compute weights $w_i = (1 - q_i)^{b^*}$
107	\STATE Return weighted loss $\mathcal{L} = \frac{\sum_i w_i \, \ell(x_i;\theta)}{\sum_i w_i}$
108	\end{algorithmic}
109	\end{algorithm}
110	
111	\subsection{Loss Reweighting vs.\ SIR Resampling}
112	
113	\citet{drayson2025contamination} implement reweighting via SIR: samples are drawn with probability proportional to $w_i$, then trained with uniform loss. We instead apply weights directly to the gradient:
114	$\nabla_\theta \mathcal{L} = \frac{1}{\sum_j w_j} \sum_i w_i \nabla_\theta \ell(x_i; \theta)$.
115	This preserves continuous, fine-grained control at the gradient level, avoids the discretization inherent in resampling, and enables per-batch $b^*$ without data preprocessing.
116	
117	%==============================================================================
118	\section{Experimental Setup}
119	\label{sec:setup}
120	%==============================================================================
121	
122	\paragraph{Data.}
123	We construct contaminated pretraining corpora from FineWeb subsets (200M tokens). At contamination rate $\rho \in \{0.4, 0.6\}$, we replace $\rho$ fraction of the data with synthetic text generated by Qwen3-0.6B (same-generator) or Llama-3.2-1B (cross-generator). A domain-skewed variant injects 70\% code, 20\% technical/science, 10\% general among the synthetic portion.
124	
125	\paragraph{Detectors.}
126	Our primary detector is TF-IDF + Logistic Regression (AUC=0.939, calibrated ECE=0.048). We also evaluate ModernBERT-150M (AUC=0.986, ECE=0.022) as a high-quality alternative.
127	
128	\paragraph{Models.}
129	We train Qwen3-0.6B and Llama-3.2-3B from pretrained checkpoints. Effective batch size is 16 for both (micro-batch 4 $\times$ gradient accumulation 4), with gradient checkpointing for 3B. Training runs 6,103 steps on 200M tokens.
130	
131	\paragraph{Baselines.}
132	\textbf{Uniform}: standard training ($b=0$). \textbf{Grid-best}: best $b$ from grid search over $\{0.1, 0.2, 0.3, 0.5, 1.0, 2.0, 5.0\}$ with 3 seeds. \textbf{Detect-and-remove}: hard filtering at $q > 0.5$. \textbf{Random reweight}: i.i.d.\ uniform $[0,1]$ weights (no detector signal).
133	
134	\paragraph{Evaluation.}
135	Primary: holdout perplexity (PPL) on 4.08M tokens. Secondary: MMLU (4-shot), HellaSwag (0-shot), Self-BLEU (4-gram). All experiments use 3 random seeds (42, 123, 456) where applicable.
136	
