194	\subsection{Contamination Rate Scaling: Effect Amplification}
195	\label{sec:rho_scaling}
196	
197	Table~\ref{tab:rho_scaling} compares $\rho=0.4$ and $\rho=0.6$ (Qwen3-0.6B, seed42). At 60\% contamination, adaptive $b^*$ improves PPL by 0.19 (1.06\% relative), a 4.75$\times$ amplification over the 0.04 PPL improvement at 40\%. Importantly, the fixed grid-best $b=0.5$ (tuned at $\rho$=0.4) transfers poorly: it captures only 47\% of the adaptive improvement at $\rho$=0.6, demonstrating that a fixed $b$ does not generalize across contamination levels.
198	
199	The $b^*$ distribution shifts rightward from median 0.87 ($\rho$=0.4) to 1.42 ($\rho$=0.6), confirming that the adaptive framework automatically increases downweighting strength as contamination increases. This $b^*$-tracks-optimum behavior provides direct evidence that $\alpha_{\mathrm{eff}}(b)$ captures the correct data-driven statistics.
