420	\section{Analysis}
421	\label{sec:analysis}
422	%==============================================================================
423	
424	\paragraph{$b^*$ tracks the optimum.}
425	The most striking finding is that the $b^*$ distribution shifts systematically with experimental conditions while requiring no manual adjustment. When contamination increases from 40\% to 60\%, median $b^*$ rises from 0.87 to 1.42---matching the shift in the grid-optimal region. When a more precise detector is used (ModernBERT), $b^*$ decreases to median 0.45 because less aggressive weighting suffices for better-separated scores. When contamination is domain-skewed, per-batch $b^*$ differentiates code-heavy batches (1.35) from general batches (0.42). These adaptations happen automatically through the $\alpha_{\mathrm{eff}}(b)$ framework, validating that it captures the correct data-driven statistics.
426	
427	\paragraph{Effect amplification addresses the small-effect concern.}
428	At 0.6B/$\rho$=0.4, the 0.23\% relative PPL improvement might appear small. However, this scales substantially: 1.06\% at $\rho$=0.6 (4.75$\times$) and 1.24\% at 3B/$\rho$=0.4 (5.39$\times$). Downstream gains of +0.7\% MMLU and +1.1\% HellaSwag on 3B further demonstrate that the cleaner gradient signal during pretraining produces measurable improvements in knowledge recall and commonsense reasoning. The value proposition is principled, zero-cost, and effective: adaptive $b^*$ is strictly better than uniform at no additional tuning cost.
429	
430	\paragraph{Lightweight detector sufficiency.}
431	TF-IDF+LR (AUC=0.939) achieves 77\% of the reweighting value of ModernBERT-150M (AUC=0.986), at negligible computational cost. This is practically significant: practitioners need not train a large neural detector to benefit from provenance-aware reweighting.
432	
433	\paragraph{Loss reweighting vs hard filtering.}
434	Detect-and-remove achieves only 55\% of the adaptive improvement while discarding 46\% of data. Under an imperfect detector (AUC $<$ 1.0), continuous weights are more tolerant of detection errors than binary decisions: a sample with $q=0.48$ (retained) and $q=0.52$ (removed) receive vastly different treatment under hard filtering but similar weights under soft reweighting.
435	
436	%==============================================================================
437	\section{Conclusion}
438	\label{sec:conclusion}
439	%==============================================================================
440	
441	We presented adaptive $b^*$, a principled method for selecting the reweighting exponent in contamination-aware language model pretraining. By deriving $b^*$ from the effective tail index $\alpha_{\mathrm{eff}}(b)$ of per-batch score distributions subject to an ESS constraint, our approach eliminates the need for validation-based grid search while matching or exceeding grid-optimal performance. The $b^*$ trajectory adapts automatically to contamination rate, model scale, detector quality, generator identity, and domain composition, confirming that the framework captures meaningful data statistics rather than requiring per-setting tuning.
442	
443	Effect amplification at higher contamination rates (4.75$\times$ at $\rho$=0.6) and larger models (5.39$\times$ at 3B) suggests that adaptive $b^*$ becomes increasingly valuable precisely where contamination matters most. The method is principled (grounded in convergence-motivated $\alpha_{\mathrm{eff}}$ estimation), zero-cost (no validation runs), and effective (consistent improvements across all tested settings).
444	
445	\paragraph{Limitations.}
446	Our experiments use controlled synthetic injection rather than organically contaminated web data; results on truly ``in the wild'' contamination remain to be validated. The method inherits the quality of the upstream detector---if the detector fails to distinguish real from synthetic text (e.g., for high-quality human-like generation), reweighting cannot help. We evaluate at up to 3B parameters and 200M tokens; scaling behavior at 7B+ and trillion-token scale is an open question.
